<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <Nullable>enable</Nullable>
    <ImplicitUsings>enable</ImplicitUsings>
    <RootNamespace>Chronicleaf.Publishers</RootNamespace>
    <AssemblyName>Chronicleaf.Publishers</AssemblyName>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The publisher contract and the approved-byte shapes, and nothing else in the repository.
      PUBLISHER-GITHUB-PAGES-ADAPTER-001/002 own one thing: how the reviewed snapshot is pushed
      to a GitHub Pages branch through the Git Data API. PUBLISHER-DEVTO-ADAPTER-001/002 own one
      thing beside it: how the reviewed snapshot becomes one Forem article through the Dev.to
      article API. Everything on the Chronicleaf side of a publish - the approval, the preview
      hash, the attempt row, the operation, the credential's revision, the audit row - belongs
      to the packages that already shipped it, so this project cannot reach any of it.

      That is why the credential arrives as a leased SecretLease handed to the one call rather
      than through Chronicleaf.Application.Secrets.ISecretVault: a project reference to the vault
      would have been the one way for a transport to start deciding things that are not its
      business, including which scope a credential belongs to. The vault-backed source of the
      seam lives in the composition root instead, and it is the only place that knows both sides.

      Repository identity arrives as two validated name segments through Chronicleaf.GitHub's
      RepoRef - owner and name, never a URL - so no value an operator or a request could supply
      can address another host.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Application/Chronicleaf.Application.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.Core/Chronicleaf.Core.csproj" />
    <ProjectReference Include="../Chronicleaf.GitHub/Chronicleaf.GitHub.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <!--
      The three abstractions a transport needs, and nothing that would make it a product feature:
      configuration to read the operator's explicit target, dependency injection to be registered
      by a composition root, and logging so a refusal can say which shape of upstream answer it
      saw. There is no HTTP client package here on purpose - the adapter takes an
      HttpMessageHandler and owns one HttpClient, so the call path is the BCL's and a test can
      substitute the far end of the wire without substituting the wire itself.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Configuration.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Logging.Abstractions" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options" Version="10.0.4" />
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.Options.ConfigurationExtensions" Version="10.0.4" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <!--
    What this project deliberately cannot reach is the point of it. There is no reference to the
    credential vault, so an adapter that wanted a plaintext key would have to invent a way to get
    one. There is no reference to the attempt store, so it cannot rewrite a receipt, and no
    reference to the domain records, so it cannot see what a milestone is. What it can do is
    render one approved snapshot to a fixed file layout, send it to one fixed origin under one
    operator-configured repository, and translate the answer.
  -->

</Project>
